521 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 129 0 R
/T (a multiplication produced a zero result without first re-examining Ac with a separate comparison instruction. This is listed as a limitation in Section 18. 13. The Three ALU Decode ROMs and How They Steer the Final Output Multiplexer The 13-bit-ALU block only understands a 2-bit Control1:Control0 select for its four basic operations. The full instruction set has sixteen opcodes doing very different things \(memory operations, branches, INC/DEC/2's- Complement, and MUL\). Rather than redesigning the ALU's own internal select logic, a decode layer sits above the ALU entirely inside the ALU project's own test-bench \(main\) circuit, built from three small ROMs, each solving one narrow sub-problem. This section traces the physical layout directly, left to right, exactly as it is laid out on the canvas.)
/E (a multiplication produced a zero result without first re-examining Ac with a separate comparison instruction. This is listed as a limitation in Section 18. 13. The Three ALU Decode ROMs and How They Steer the Final Output Multiplexer The 13-bit-ALU block only understands a 2-bit Control1:Control0 select for its four basic operations. The full instruction set has sixteen opcodes doing very different things \(memory operations, branches, INC/DEC/2's- Complement, and MUL\). Rather than redesigning the ALU's own internal select logic, a decode layer sits above the ALU entirely inside the ALU project's own test-bench \(main\) circuit, built from three small ROMs, each solving one narrow sub-problem. This section traces the physical layout directly, left to right, exactly as it is laid out on the canvas.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
